# 5. AUTHENTICATION AND ROLE PROTECTION

Implement two roles only:

- Admin
- Employee

### Admin

Admin can:
- Manage company settings.
- Add, edit, and delete employees.
- View company attendance.
- Approve or reject leave requests.
- View company dashboards.
- View employee payslips.
- Post announcements.
- Use the helper on company data.

### Employee

Employee can:
- Log in and log out.
- View their personal dashboard.
- Check in and check out.
- View their own attendance.
- Apply for leave.
- View their own leave balance and request history.
- View their own payslip.
- Read announcements.
- Ask questions about their own data.

### Access enforcement

Every page must check the active session and role before rendering protected content.

If an Employee attempts to open an Admin page, redirect them to their own dashboard. If no valid session exists, redirect to the login page.

Also enforce authorization inside JavaScript functions that read or modify data. Hiding navigation items is not sufficient.

Use employee IDs to scope personal records, not just the email address or a user-supplied URL parameter.

When adding an employee, automatically create the corresponding login account. Validate email uniqueness across all users.

Support logout and a change-password flow if feasible within the scope of the brief.

**Security qualification:** because the project uses only browser storage and has no backend, role protection is appropriate for the learning demonstration but cannot be considered secure against a technically capable user who can edit local storage or JavaScript.

Do not falsely claim server-side security or production-grade authentication.

